// oob_loop_overflow — Loop goes one past array end
// Expected exit code: 134
// Compile: compile_dump <file> <out> <backend> --opt-level=O3
//
// Bounds SAFE-ONLY negative test: allocate a 4-byte buffer
// (`[u8; 4]`) and loop `i` over the closed range [0, 4] (using
// `i <= 4` as the loop condition — a classic off-by-one in the loop
// bound). Each iteration writes `i` to `b.data[i]`. With bounds-check
// IR (always on in VUMA 2.0), on the final iteration (i == 4) the
// injected `UGe(i, 4)` bounds check evaluates to true (4 >= 4) and
// dispatches to `__oob_trap`, which aborts with exit code 134
// (SIGABRT, 128 + 6).
//
// IMPORTANT: This test is NOT in the regular `bounds_wave1/` gold
// standard suite because it only passes with bounds-check IR injected
// (always on in VUMA 2.0). Without bounds-check IR (legacy mode), the
// OOB write corrupts adjacent memory and behaviour is undefined. The
// expected exit code below assumes bounds-check IR is injected.
//
// VUMA Key Concepts:
//   - state_new(Layout) allocation (PMT idiom)
//   - `[u8; 4]` array field with a known 4-byte size
//   - while-loop with `i <= 4` (closed range) — exercises the
//     bounds check inside a loop body, where the index `i` is a
//     phi-merged SSA value that the optimiser cannot fold away
//   - exit 134 == SIGABRT (128 + 6) emitted by `__oob_trap`.

layout Buf = { data: [u8; 4] }

transform main() -> i32 {
    let b = state_new(Buf);
    let i = 0;
    // Loop bound is `<= 4` — on the i==4 iteration the Store traps
    while i <= 4 {
        b.data[i] = i;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
